[" Here's a solution using the `MutationObserver` API for handling dynamic updates, taking Shadow Root into account, considering virtual DOM libraries like React or Vue.js, optimizing for performance, supporting asynchronous data loading, maintaining cross-browser compatibility, and scaling with large DOM trees.

```javascript
function traverseDOM(selector, callback, root = document.body) {
  let visitElement = (el) => {
    if (el && el !== document.documentingElement) {
      const isMatch = el.matches ? el.matches(selector) : el.classList.contains(selector);
      if (isMatch) callback(el);

      // Iterate through children and shadow roots, recursively visiting each child element.
      [].slice.call(el.children).forEach(visitElement);
      const shadows = Array.from(el.shadowRoots || []).flatMap(node => Array.from(node.children));
      shadows.forEach(visitElement);
    }
  };

  // Create a MutationObserver to handle DOM updates
  const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
    mutations.forEach(({ addedNodes, removedNodes }) => {
      if (addedNodes && addedNodes.length > 0) {
        Array.from(addedNodes).forEach((node) => visitElement(node));
      }
      if (removedNodes && removedNodes.length > 0) {
        Array.from(removedNodes).forEach((node) => {
          const existing = root.contains(node);
          if (existing) root.removeChild(node);
        });
      }
    });
  });

  // Start observing the DOM tree
  observer.observe(root, { childList: true, subtree: true });

  visitElement(root);
}
```

This solution uses a recursive function `visitElement()` to traverse the DOM tree and callback to evaluate elements based on their attributes or class names. The `MutationObserver` API is utilized to handle dynamic updates, considering both the addition and removal of elements during traversal. Shadow Roots are taken into account by iterating through them recursively.

For virtual DOM libraries, this function should work seamlessly since it relies on standard DOM APIs. Regarding performance, the solution utilizes a flat map to handle shadow roots and uses `Array.from()` to convert NodeLists to arrays for easier iteration. Asynchronous data loading can be accommodated by ensuring that all elements are populated before triggering the traversal function.

To maintain cross-browser compatibility, this solution relies on modern browser features with fallbacks or polyfills where necessary. Lastly, the scalability of this solution is ensured by leveraging asynchronous DOM traversal and efficient data structures like arrays to store and iterate through elements."]

