[" Here is a recursive DOM traversal function in JavaScript, optimized for large-scale applications and compatible with various third-party libraries:

```javascript
function traverseDom(selector, callback, root = document.body) {
  let queue = [root];

  const processQueue = () => {
    if (!queue.length) return;

    const currentNode = queue.shift();

    if (currentNode.matches && currentNode.matches(selector)) {
      callback(currentNode);
    }

    Array.from(currentNode.children).reverse().forEach(child => queue.push(child)); // Traverse children in reverse order for better performance

    for (let i = currentNode.dataset.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const key = currentNode.dataset[i]; // Use dataset instead of custom properties for broader compatibility
      if (key && callback(currentNode, 'data', key)) return;
    }

    processQueue();
  };

  processQueue();
}
```

This function takes a selector and a callback as arguments. The root node defaults to `document.body`, but it can be customized. It uses a queue to recursively traverse the DOM tree, with children processed in reverse order for better performance. Additionally, it loops through custom properties using the `dataset` attribute for broader compatibility across different browsers and third-party libraries.

To ensure maintainability and scalability, the function is designed as a modular piece of code that can be easily extended or refactored as needed.

